592186526197 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186526175 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526197 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526197 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x,y)=- 2 x^{2} + 3 x y + 4 y^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526197 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9ba2b920-eb38-5aa2-a748-ae3fcff5a4ef" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526197 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=- 4 x + 3 y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3 x + 8 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =3 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =8 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =(3 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+(8 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}) \\\\  & =(3 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 4 r \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)})+(8 r \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}) \\\\  & =8 r \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 6 r \\s